this video is sponsored by  curiositystream  if you've seen dune already this clip  probably feels different from what you  remember  because i removed the score  [Music]  i've seen many people talk about the  film's imagery and it's very impressive  but the first thing that greets you in  this movie before you even see the  studio logos is sound  and i think both the score and the sound  design play a huge role in making denis  villeneuve's new adaptation of dune the  experience that it is i had a great time  watching dune and walking out of the  theater one of the things i was most  excited about was the film's score the  sound design and hans zimmer score add a  huge amount of scale and texture to the  world it's wild and completely unlike  anything else i've heard some of the  sounds he created for this score are  truly kind of terrifying  it uses layers and layers of voices  singing  chanting  whispering  yelling  throat singing  along with sounds from instruments that  genuinely look like they could have come  from getty prime  i fell in love with the score and what  it brought to the film so naturally i  wanted to watch doom without it  by controlling which of the surround  sound channels are active in a video  editor we can get a sense of what a  scene would be like without the dialogue  or without the score so i watched  through a bunch of scenes with just the  score no dialogue and just the dialogue  and no score to see what i could learn  about the score and the role it plays in  the film  for dune's score zimmer wanted to invoke  sounds that felt like they came from a  place thousands of years in the future  he didn't want the audience to ever hear  a full orchestra and instead wanted to  focus on using sounds that felt like  they came from the world of doom  typically in a score you might have a  melodic theme or a motif represent a  character or a place but for this  adaptation of dune zimmer leans away  from that he definitely uses certain  melodic or rhythmic themes  like the rhythmic drum hit  repeated throughout the film  in a variety of ways often prefacing  action or conflict  but zimmer focuses more on connecting  certain kinds of sounds to groups  influences or settings and then using  those sounds to invoke those things he  relates the insane grinding rhythmic  throb with the harkenin  he uses this whispery female chanting  for the betty jesuit  house of tradies gets more traditional  warmer sounds  i told my father i didn't want this  either so while duncan idaho has his own  little theme it also has a sonic  connection to the sound zimmer is using  for house of tradies as a whole  [Music]  and the desert and the fremen get higher  pitch sounds that invoke wind and sand  wind instruments like flutes as well as  vocalization that is distinct from the  whispery high-pitched singing of the  benny jesuit  [Music]  so this is where it gets really  brilliant i think dune is a complicated  story with a lot of different things  going on there are many competing forces  paul as a character is struggling  between different identities and  callings and associating different  sounds and instruments with different  ideas allows zimmer to actually bring  these different forces into conflict  within a single piece or melody  for example once we associate this sound  with the benny jesuit  and this kind of sound with the etredes  and the duke  listen to how these two ideas come into  play in the score during this  conversation alongside the dialogue  will you protect our son  with my life i'm not asking his mother  i'm asking the bene jesuit  when the duke addresses jessica as the  benny jesuit we hear the benny jesuit  sounds come in  will you protect paul  and then when she tries to reassure him  we hear the house atreides sound come in  but it comes in on top of the  benedezurite sound if zimmer had been  relying here on a melody to communicate  these two ideas this would have been a  lot more difficult but instead both can  exist within the same piece  simultaneously  another example is this scene the higher  pitched dentist drill sound represents  the pain being inflicted on paul's hand  by the box and the vocalizations  represent something being awakened deep  within paul these two forces can  actually exist and come into conflict  within the peace  so we have the desert the benny jesuit  the fremen house of tradies and various  prophecies represented in the score and  paul struggles internally between all  these roles and identities  [Music]  in the book we can actually read this  play out in his internal monologue but  in the film they decided to stay away  from any kind of voice over and so the  score helps bring that kind of  internality onto the screen and that's  not to say the score isn't utilizing  melodic themes but zimmer instead of  linking melodies to specific characters  with the exception of maybe duncan idaho  uses them more to illustrate plot lines  there are complementary melodic motifs  that follow paul throughout his journey  into the desert and because zimmer is  using sound for locations and groups  instead of melody he can evolve how he's  presenting that melody depending on  where paul is in his journey for example  we're first introduced to this theme  that follows paul's journey into the  desert with the warmer tones used for  house of tradies but there's also a  sense of mournfulness and longing as he  awakens from dreams of the planet  arrakis and the desert and then when  paul is learning about the fremen and  the desert on arrakis we get this same  theme but it's a bit distorted as if  we're only getting a vague picture of  the place  finally when paul is flying over the  desert we get the theme again but this  time it's no longer distorted it has a  new intensity and it's lacking that long  quality as if to say  this is my new home this melody marks a  certain element the plot and the way  it's portrayed sonically gives that plot  an emotional arc  zimmer uses another complimentary  melodic motif to represent pieces of  this same journey into the desert  look at how this moment  is linked to this one with both a visual  and a melodic motif  [Music]  while the soundscape of voices and  instruments that zimmer uses for each  moment represents the two environments  distinctly  these two complementary themes come  together in the final scene as paul  advances even deeper into the desert the  track for leaving calidan and the film's  climax are very similar but while paul  is in kaladan the vocals play more of a  background role while the track leans  more heavily on the aggressive  bagpipe style sounds that were  associating with house atreides but for  the film's climax in the desert those  sounds are still there but they fall to  the background while the vocals take the  lead  another example of how this plays out is  with the benny desert order while they  don't spend much time on screen they  play a huge role in the story behind the  scenes they're not visually present in  the destruction of arakin but we know  from the story they've played a part in  orchestrating this betrayal  so i love that their influence on these  events is felt through this scene  because of their inclusion in the score  similarly when the hunter seeker shows  up we're clued into the fact that it  comes from the harkonnens through the  score  before their involvement is revealed  through the dialogue the hawking agent  was cemented into that hole six weeks  ago  let's talk about duncan idaho and his  theme for a moment as i mentioned he's  one of the few characters that gets a  very blatant melodic theme  [Music]  but i think that this theme is used in a  clever way  almost every time duncan shows up we  hear his theme but when he's sacrificing  himself to defeat the sardikar instead  of hearing his theme which would seem  like the obvious move here we get a nod  to the fremen musically  and then when paul is going into combat  with one of the fremen we actually hear  duncan's theme  and i think this helps to illustrate the  interconnectedness of everything duncan  has learned from the fremen and he uses  that knowledge here in defeating the  sardicar and duncan's theme here is a  nod to the fact that paul has learned to  fight from duncan even though we never  actually see that in the film now i will  say if i have a critique here at all  it's that i think the score might be a  little overused in some moments i think  it's a brilliantly constructed score i  love how it sounds but there aren't many  scenes without any score at all watching  some of these scenes especially more  intimate dialogue scenes without the  score made me feel like it might have  been better to play some of these  moments with a little less score  ever since you brought him before your  reverend mother he hasn't been the same  he's distracted the film in many ways  feels kind of like it's rushing through  a bunch of setup when you drop the score  out of some of these scenes it slows  things down and gives the characters a  little more intimacy and room to breathe  and ironically i think doing this would  actually make the film score even more  impactful when it is used because the  intensity would only be magnified in  contrast to the quieter more intimate  scenes  but that's more of an issue with sound  editing than it is with the score i  think it's an incredible and inventive  score one that for me doesn't just help  make the film more exciting but actually  adds complexity to the story like denny  villeneuve han zimmer has been a  longtime fan of the book and i think his  knowledge of the material shows through  his work it's not just a score that  sounds like it comes from a distant  world in the far future it's a score  that takes the book's metaphysical and  mystical elements which take a backseat  in the dialogue and exposition in the  film and weaves them into the story  for me the success of this score is the  way it adds to the dramatic conflict of  the story and the way it takes part in  the world building sounding like it's an  artifact of the world itself  while watching the film to dissect the  score i discovered some cool ways sound  design and mixing are used in the film  like how they got the baron's voice to  just sound so terrifying  so i've decided to make a companion  video to this one that breaks those  elements down you can watch it right now  on nebula nebula is a streaming service  built and owned by creators and the best  way to get nebula and access to that  video is with the curiosity stream  nebula bundle which you can get for less  than 15 a year when you sign up using my  link below on nebula you can see bonus  exclusive content from me like my  breakdown of dude sound design or my  video about omar little's code from the  wire but you'll also get access to all  my usual videos early and without any  ads or sponsors when you sign up for the  bundle using my link you'll also get  access to curiosity stream where you'll  find a ton of educational and  documentary content if you're interested  in seeing very real stunning desert  visuals you could check out the  documentary africa's desert storms on  curiosity stream right now this bundle  is an amazing deal for less than fifteen  15 for an entire year you'll get access  to two streaming services full of  amazing content signing up and watching  on nebula helps support myself and other  independent creators like me click the  link on the screen to sign up now and  watch my video on dunes sound design  [Music]  [Music]

